A two-way prop the caller does not bind is state of the instance: a cell
initialised from the value the call gave the prop, or its default, and written
by the host's reports, as if the component declared a `var` for it.

`checkbox #box(label="alone")` binds nothing, so its cell starts at the
default and the text reading `box.checked` reads the cell. `checked=true` is a
one-way value, which says where the cell starts rather than holding the
widget to it. Under the `for`, each row's checkbox is an instance with a cell
of its own -- the inliner treats the checkbox as it treats a component with a
`var`: a record on fyne and gtk4, a factory on html, a cell per copy on
bubbletea, `remember` on android.

Before this, an unbound checkbox reported its clicks into nothing, and a read
of `box.checked` reached every Go and Kotlin emitter as a field of the widget
that nothing declares.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `m\.box\b` -- the read names the cell, not a Model field nothing declares
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `m\.box\.Checked` -- the read names the cell, not a field of a GTK handle
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `\bbox\.checked` -- the read names the remembered cell

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
        bubbletea
    }
    none { html }
    kotlin { android }
}

component main node {
    var items = ["a", "b"]

    vbox {
        checkbox #box(label="alone")
        text(value=box.checked ? "on" : "off")
        checkbox(checked=true, label="starts on")
        for var it = items {
            checkbox(label=it)
        }
    }
}

window {
    main
}
